WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.813
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.813 --> 00:00:07.211
So I thought we'd do two
main sets of things.

00:00:07.211 --> 00:00:10.860
I want to talk to you about your signature
culture, because I think it is so

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.590
interesting to all of us.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.250
And then at some point we'll pivot from
that into talking a little bit more

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:21.190
generally about leadership, leadership
style, and how you approach leadership,

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.970
and then we'll leave some time for
questions from the audience and

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:26.810
we'll wrap up at the end.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:33.340
So, talk to me about some of these
signature pieces of the culture

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:38.719
that I refer to in my opening remarks, the
idea of mayor and village and teammates.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.130
The dress up,
the chanting that I heard backstage.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:47.817
What's the story behind all of this?

00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:52.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we believe we're
a community first and a company second.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:53.850
And hopefully,

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.970
everybody has some communities that
they're a part of In their life.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:00.350
It could be their family, could be
their book club, could be their church,

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.640
could be their sports team,
could be their best friends.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.950
Community basically means that people care
about each other, respect each other and

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.720
they care about the organization
that they are apart of.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.660
That they're stewards that care about
what they give on to the next generation.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.760
That's the basic concept of community.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.430
And so we believe in that fervently
that given we spend more than 50% of

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:24.780
our waking hours as an adult at work,
and for some of us,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.190
it's way more than 50%, especially if you
travel, that to concede that in any way

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:33.900
that shouldn't be central to your life
mission, and that you shouldn't be

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.050
emotionally engaged with the people
that you work with all that time.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:42.307
We think it's a grotesque concession
that some people have to make.

00:01:42.307 --> 00:01:44.605
And a lot of people don't have to.

00:01:44.605 --> 00:01:49.078
And people like the ones in this room are
going to have a spectacularly big impact

00:01:49.078 --> 00:01:53.040
on the folks that work for
you through the course of your life.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.970
And so we said, okay we're going to
be a community, act like a community,

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.820
let's call it a village.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.580
Let's make it the mayor because that
raises the bar of expected behavior

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:03.790
for us.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:08.720
And so we take it very seriously when
we make decisions, we consistently use

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.460
the metaphor, if your a Mayor of
a village, how would you decide?

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.110
Because if you're a mayor of a village,
you care about the economy, but

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:17.420
the economy is the means to an end,
it's not the end.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:21.630
You want to help the economy so you can
have good schools, good parks, etc.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.890
&gt;&gt; So, I have to say, I mean I alluded to
what happened in the green room, before.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.360
But it is very impressive.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.760
So here you are meeting up with
a bunch of folks from, you know,

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.320
the local area, who,
you don't do this every day.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.100
And yet, you were able to
lead them through a series of

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.047
cheers which are clearly deeply ingrained.

00:02:41.047 --> 00:02:45.140
And done as you can see, by just
looking around the room, with you know,

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:48.270
with a sense of passion and by in.

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.980
Which of course is critical to having,
we can talk about culture all we like.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:58.050
But getting it bought in in that way
deeply through a fortune 500 company.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:59.480
I mean that's tough.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.030
So could you tell us a little bit about,
how do you go about that?

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:02.580
How do you do that?

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.470
&gt;&gt; Well first, teammates stand up.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:07.480
What the heck.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.205
I got some teammates here.

00:03:09.205 --> 00:03:10.476
So.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:03:10.476 --> 00:03:14.029
&gt;&gt; I knew there were going to be a couple

00:03:14.029 --> 00:03:20.390
here, but I didn't know that
there was going to be a bunch.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.460
And so
this is a pleasant surprise right before.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.040
So I will ask them three questions.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:25.890
Stay standing.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:28.480
What is this company?

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:29.830
&gt;&gt; You.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:30.740
&gt;&gt; Whose company is it?

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:31.502
&gt;&gt; Ours.

00:03:31.502 --> 00:03:32.594
&gt;&gt; And what can it be?

00:03:32.594 --> 00:03:33.569
&gt;&gt; Special.

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:36.671
&gt;&gt; And if we are to build the greatest
health care community the world has ever

00:03:36.671 --> 00:03:38.283
seen, let it be like this one for all!

00:03:38.283 --> 00:03:39.514
&gt;&gt; All for one.

00:03:39.514 --> 00:03:40.559
&gt;&gt; Damn straight!

00:03:40.559 --> 00:03:47.819
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; So

00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:52.380
this part wasn't conscious when
we started this adventure.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.790
And it kind of popped up organically.

00:03:54.790 --> 00:04:02.580
And what we find is that healthy tribes,
military units, religions, sports teams.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.250
Places that have strong sense of team,

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:07.210
strong sense of community,
they do call and response.

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:08.280
A language means something.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.450
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Rituals mean something.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.240
And so
we said if we're going to be a community,

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:16.220
we should have the attributes of
a healthy religion, tribe, village.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:20.620
And it turns out call and responses is
what happens naturally in those quarters.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.550
Doesn't happen naturally
in most companies.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:23.864
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; Because they're sort of

00:04:23.864 --> 00:04:24.575
morally sterile.

00:04:24.575 --> 00:04:29.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
No value judgment implied.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.925
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
[LAUGH] I said sort of.

00:04:31.925 --> 00:04:37.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So it is

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.020
pretty amazing to watch, but
how do you know it works?

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.700
Obviously, it works in that
you're able to perpetuate it, but

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.710
how do you know that you're
distinctive because of it?

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:52.240
How do you know that you're getting
superior results because of it?

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:53.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we're very metric oriented.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:56.331
And if you're going to be
serious about being a community,

00:04:56.331 --> 00:04:58.280
you've got to be objective.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:03.860
And we certainly, not all of our
neighborhoods are happy neighborhoods, but

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:08.230
our clinical outcomes
are superior to our competition.

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:12.284
The government just came out with a new
rating system, for example only 30% of

00:05:12.284 --> 00:05:16.518
the centers in America, in dialysis, which
is one of our two big businesses, can be

00:05:16.518 --> 00:05:20.571
ranked at four or five, and the percentage
of our centers that are in those four or

00:05:20.571 --> 00:05:23.220
five categories is twice
as high as any competitor.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.960
So it's a crushing example.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:28.930
In addition we did an acquisition,
about a three billion dollar acquisition,

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:30.300
x years ago.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.760
We did all the analysis, our wages were
the same, our salaries were the same.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:36.127
We were in all the same
geographies across America.

00:05:36.127 --> 00:05:38.263
Their turnover was 40% higher.

00:05:38.263 --> 00:05:43.182
So if we were them we would have had to
hire an extra 1,200 people a year compared

00:05:43.182 --> 00:05:47.540
to what we had to do given the difference
in retention at the same pay across

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:48.200
America.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.970
And third there's some of the big
consulting firms the Ann's,

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:54.640
the Hewitt's that do standard
questions across big companies.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:57.520
And our team mate engagement
scores are quite high, and

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:59.180
then I'll give one anecdote.

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:02.570
About five, six years in, a woman came
up to me at a big conference, and said,

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:06.990
they've been hired by a pharmaceutical
company to do focus groups with patients.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:09.780
And you know they had never worked for
us, they were never going to work for us,

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:14.550
and she said by the third city,
we were playing a game,

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:15.880
because that's when you
have a facilitator,

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:19.650
and then the sponsors are behind a mirror,
looking at the people, and

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:23.100
then the 15 patients answering the
questions, just like they do with cars and

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.610
toothpaste and all that, and
by the third city, we played a game that

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:31.140
about 40 minutes in, since they didn't ask
the question of do you know who owns and

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:34.860
operates your dialysis center, until
the very end, about 40 minutes into it,

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.590
they would play a game and guess which
patients were the Devita patients.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:40.500
Because you could tell that
they talked differently.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.870
And this, at this point was across
a thousand different locations,

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:45.840
small locations across America.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:48.927
That was, for us,
separate from all those hard metrics,

00:06:48.927 --> 00:06:53.160
that was perhaps the most special one,
that human beings talked differently.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:57.150
&gt;&gt; You spoke a little bit
about some acquisitions and

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:02.410
spreading culture through acquisitions and
the one that you referred to is one where

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:06.910
it was really a like business model that
you were taking the culture and too bad.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:10.910
And of course,
a couple years ago you bought HBC,

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:14.800
which is in a quite different,
related but different business.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.420
So have you tried to take
the same culture there?

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:23.120
Are there limits to the extent to
which you can transfer the culture to?

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.110
&gt;&gt; Both the big dialysis company,
when we bought them,

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:31.920
they were horrified at the notion of
being subjected to this weird shit.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.890
Yeah, that they said it looks like a cult.

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:36.730
&gt;&gt; That's a technical term.

00:07:36.730 --> 00:07:37.338
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:07:37.338 --> 00:07:40.340
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; A term, it's a, I'm just quoting them.

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:44.120
The, that this seems like a cult.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:45.640
It seems sophomoric.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:46.314
It seems cheesy.

00:07:46.314 --> 00:07:50.244
It seems inappropriate,
cavalier, and both there and

00:07:50.244 --> 00:07:55.176
then with this related business
three years ago, they were similarly

00:07:55.176 --> 00:07:59.710
worried about being subjected to it,
forced to try to adopt it.

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:03.329
We made very clear in rooms like this,
only not as nice,

00:08:03.329 --> 00:08:05.872
we can't afford nice rooms like this.

00:08:05.872 --> 00:08:08.681
They make very clear,
please don't use our language, unless or

00:08:08.681 --> 00:08:11.040
until it becomes comfortable.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:15.110
Don't talk about, don't feel like
you have to talk about our values or

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:16.591
some of our concepts for the village.

00:08:16.591 --> 00:08:18.517
Don't do any of that, get to know us.

00:08:18.517 --> 00:08:22.370
Watch how we behave, watch how we
interact, and then a year downstream,

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:26.210
we'll talk about whether or not we need
to change any of the values or whatever.

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:29.590
And both the first time and
second time, legacy

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:34.100
to be the veterans were appalled that
I would say that we might change them.

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:37.160
But my response was, if you're a village,
you have to represent the current

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:41.810
population, and when you have a lot
of new people, it's a new thing.

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:43.500
And you need to speak for
that new community and

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:48.330
in the case of this related acquisition,
that from a business point of view,

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:50.940
the first two years went very poorly.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:52.410
Tremendous organizational change,

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:56.960
we took out half the senior executives, so
relatively traumatized organization which

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:00.820
exasperated the fear around
one hell of a village.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:04.260
You let half the people go and
you're introducing all this change.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:09.620
And so in the second nationwide meeting
since the time we closed the deal,

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:13.060
once a year we get our top
4,000 leaders or so together,

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:17.790
I went off in a room with the 270 leaders
from that enterprise, 18 months in,

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:21.850
traumatic 18 months, and said, okay,
now we're going to have a debate.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:27.090
Do you want to go adopt the legacy
DaVita vision, mission and values?

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:31.170
Do you want to keep what you have,
wait another year, keep it forever, or

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:32.580
somewhere in between?

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:36.950
And it was a very intense debate.

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:40.770
About 80 out of that 250 people or
so were physicians.

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:45.270
People from all over America somewhere new
to the enterprise because the acquisitions

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:48.020
had been in post flow,
some of them had been there 25 years.

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:53.860
They went through it, and about
halfway through a woman stood up, and

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:56.620
it was a very intense debate, a woman
stood up, and one thing we didn't have on

00:09:56.620 --> 00:10:01.060
the ballot was go all in and
take the vision mission and values.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.790
You can take these two,
these two-thirds or just one or none.

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:07.690
Because we never thought it would go all
in, but this one woman stood up and said,

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:12.160
you know what, it's been a year and
a half since the deal.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:15.650
We have wonderful people in our company,

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:20.270
health care partners, they have
wonderful people in legacy, DaVita.

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:23.520
They are living the values
that we all agree with,

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.950
with far more intentionality and
follow through and

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:30.180
purpose and robustness than we are,
and I want to be one company.

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:33.240
I vote for all in, I think that
should be added to the ballot.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:35.080
Applause broke out in the room.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:38.630
We had another hour of debate,
both in small groups and big groups and

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:40.510
at the end of that two and a half hours.

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:47.150
That 260 or so leaders from that
enterprise voted 85% to go all in.

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:51.060
14 of the remaining 15 voted to
go two- thirds of the way in.

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:57.015
Only 1% voted, so they voted for,
embraced the village concept themselves,

00:10:57.015 --> 00:11:00.250
much to, I think, to their own surprise.

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:03.550
No one knew what the outcome was going to
be going into that room, and therefore,

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:06.910
there was no one subjected to anything,
and they went out and

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:08.440
told everybody across America that.

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:12.770
I've sort of taken this
stuff more seriously now and

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:15.040
in fact I just voted for it.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:16.930
&gt;&gt; And, has it taken root?

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:21.920
I'd say the math I use as I
think we're one third teammates,

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:24.882
citizens, maybe more, maybe 33 to 40%.

00:11:24.882 --> 00:11:30.580
We're about 30 to 35% in
a just still watching,

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:35.100
observing and whatever number
is left are still negative.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:41.420
&gt;&gt; So, still on the subject
of exporting the culture,

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:45.260
you've taken DaVita now internationally.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:48.390
Can you talk a little bit about
some of the challenges, and

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:53.510
taking this kind of corporate
culture to international cultures?

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:57.360
&gt;&gt; This has been very intense.

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:00.950
So first,
when you think about core values,

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:04.910
some people will look at them on a wall
and say, it's motherhood and apple pie.

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:06.230
Who doesn't believe in integrity?

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:07.150
Who doesn't believe in team?

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:09.980
What a dumb, pointless list,
everybody believes in them.

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:14.050
And as a leader the issue is not belief,
it's practice.

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:15.620
So yeah, I believe in all those things.

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:19.730
If you evaluate exactly how I behave
everyday some people would say, hey KT,

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:23.385
I'm not so sure that you're living
that core value that you believe in.

00:12:23.385 --> 00:12:27.270
And even harder to create an environment
where everybody practices that

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:30.660
an not violating a core value
does not equal living it.

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:34.740
So if I go a month without telling a lie
that is not living a life of integrity.

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:37.830
Living a life of integrity is when you
tell the truth when it hurts when its

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:40.010
embarrassing when you didn't have to.

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:43.740
If i go a month without blaming
someone for something they didn't do,

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:46.070
I'm not living a code
value of accountability.

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:49.400
Living code value of accountability
is when someone makes a mistake and

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:50.620
no one thinks you had
anything to do with it.

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:54.050
And you realize that you knew
the person wasn't very good about that.

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:55.900
You could have taken
them aside two weeks for

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:58.350
half an hour,
they never would have made the mistake.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:00.980
And you stand up and take accountability
because you said, you know what,

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:04.620
I could have prevented that,
I know Josh was uncertain or whatever.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.510
And so there's a big difference
between not violating and

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:10.930
living, and
it's contagious how that works.

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:15.310
And so internationally, when we decided
to go, if I was a better CEO we would

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:19.850
have gone international ten years ago, but
I wasn't, so we started four years ago.

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:24.280
And I went to other CEO's from prestigious
business schools like Stanford,

00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:29.455
and they all said, you know that weird
stuff you do, leave it at the airport.

00:13:29.455 --> 00:13:31.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; because, it's unusual,

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:36.200
even in America, so don't you dare
try to do it in Saudi Arabia,

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:38.572
don't you dare try and
do it in China, etc.

00:13:38.572 --> 00:13:46.230
Poland, Portugal, Columbia, India,
all these other places we are.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:51.240
So I took their advice, and then I was
giving a talk to a bunch of our Indian

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:56.120
teammates x months later as we begin
to grow and it just fell totally flat.

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:57.530
And so I went to the boss after,

00:13:57.530 --> 00:14:00.690
I knew him enough where he was
honest to give me that gift.

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:05.860
I said well that really fell flat, and
he said, they've all been on the Internet.

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:09.410
If you go walk around their cubicles,
they've got the DeVita symbols up.

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:13.600
They've got the story of how our
neighborhood has grown within the context.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:18.120
And now they're feeling like this village
thing, that's just about America and

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:22.107
they're not really citizens,
they're just foreign laborers.

00:14:22.107 --> 00:14:26.160
And so I voice-mailed back,
we went full on, and

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:30.997
in doing that,
you find out that it's about human values.

00:14:30.997 --> 00:14:34.913
That everyone wants to have a robust
discussion around actual human values and

00:14:34.913 --> 00:14:38.960
whether or not they're used in making
day-to-day business decisions or not.

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:42.420
It's not a fairy tale thing, and

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:46.320
we've had uncomfortable moments in some
of the cultures, as we work through.

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:49.570
In particular, for example,
because we won't stand for

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:53.330
some of the gender discrimination that
exists in a lot of other societies,

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:55.090
and that leads to awkwardness.

00:14:55.090 --> 00:14:58.650
And then one other moment of awkwardness,
and then I'll stop.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:01.560
So you're trying to get the right
translations is not always

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:02.170
straight forward.

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:07.650
And so it turns out in one country,

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:12.245
the word that we were using for
fulfillment, basically means masturbation.

00:15:12.245 --> 00:15:15.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And, and so

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:17.665
we were saying,
you know fulfillment's very important.

00:15:17.665 --> 00:15:21.780
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We think everyone should be fulfilled.

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:24.915
You know even we hold our leaders
accountable for fulfillment.

00:15:24.915 --> 00:15:28.980
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And then someone gave the speaker,

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:30.830
it was not me, the gift.

00:15:30.830 --> 00:15:32.456
So there are speed bumps.

00:15:32.456 --> 00:15:36.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In the way.

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:37.550
&gt;&gt; And you had metrics to track.

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:38.205
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes, right.

00:15:38.205 --> 00:15:41.044
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I mean, probably trivia

00:15:41.044 --> 00:15:45.406
of your days have been, but when we
teach strategy we talk of course about

00:15:45.406 --> 00:15:49.641
alignment between organization,
including culture and strategy.

00:15:49.641 --> 00:15:54.122
But then we also talk about alignment
between that and the excellent

00:15:54.122 --> 00:15:59.152
environment that you find yourself in,
industry and so on, so the question

00:15:59.152 --> 00:16:04.530
really is How general do you think the
applicability of this kind of culture is?

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:09.100
Is this something that works
in mission driven healthcare,

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:10.770
mission driven kinds of industries?

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:14.480
Is this something that you think
could applied more broadly?

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:15.680
What are the limits?

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:19.623
&gt;&gt; This is one where we're quite intense
because a lot of people will kind of go,

00:16:19.623 --> 00:16:22.890
oh they're nice little
health care service people.

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:25.740
And I'm sure very pleasant to talk to,

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:28.490
but they probably lose
all their football games.

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:32.630
And we over the last 16
years are in the top 5%

00:16:32.630 --> 00:16:34.750
of New York Stock Exchange performers.

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:37.590
We outperform our competition
in clinical outcomes.

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:40.230
And a lot of our teammates
have gone on to be COs and

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:41.740
COOs of lots of other companies.

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:47.460
So we don't think there's any tension
between achieving capitalistic and

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:50.140
market based net worth ambitions, and

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:53.230
creating a healthy and
distinctive community for people to work.

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:55.820
We think there's no tension between those.

00:16:55.820 --> 00:17:00.100
In fact, we think they're in some ways
cohabitate very positively and so

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:04.970
if I was running an enterprise
with 3,000 Taco Bells,

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:08.850
not medical clinics or
a coal mine, or any,

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:12.240
it is all about creating
a differentially healthy environment for

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:16.150
people to work, particularly the people
that don't have the career options and

00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:21.190
income flexibility that most
of us in this room have.

00:17:21.190 --> 00:17:24.150
That, that applies to any business.

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:28.200
That we, a healthy workplace
is a gift to that mommy or

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:30.260
that daddy that they will take home.

00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:33.390
It is a gift that they will savor at work.

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:36.610
And it's about basic ways in which
humans behave with one another.

00:17:36.610 --> 00:17:41.270
So we believe passionately,
that it's transferable everywhere.

00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:44.490
That's not to say that there aren't
differences in how one would implement,

00:17:44.490 --> 00:17:47.240
how it would manifest itself
in a Goldman Sachs, or

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:52.740
a Bain company,
versus the other organizations

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:58.380
that the demographics of
the citizenship does affect it.

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:03.590
But the basic principles have to do
with how we behave with one another.

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:08.030
And whether or not it's consistent with
the values that we allege to hold dear.

00:18:09.990 --> 00:18:13.640
&gt;&gt; And so I'd like to start to
transition to leadership and

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:17.910
keep one foot in culture and
put one foot in leadership.

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:23.830
So some of the things that
we're talking about village,

00:18:23.830 --> 00:18:27.490
mayor, community, sounds soft.

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:28.230
They sound soft.

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:29.929
They sound a little bit squishy.

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:32.730
Very GSPS actually.

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:35.890
&gt;&gt; I wasn't going to say it.

00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:41.200
&gt;&gt; We're also heavily into costumes,
which you call uniforms.

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:43.710
So see a parallel there as well.

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:49.790
But at the end of the day,
you're the CEO of a Fortune 500 company.

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:52.340
And that's a tough role.

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:54.790
It requires tough decision making.

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:57.400
It requires holding people accountable.

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:00.920
You know, that doesn't sound so
soft and squishy actually.

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:05.660
So I'm just wondering how much of this
translates into your day to day job?

00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:07.650
How do you think about that?

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:10.070
&gt;&gt; And Elizabeth at some
point the video is ready.

00:19:10.070 --> 00:19:11.610
We can play it.

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:15.280
Okay, didn't okay.

00:19:18.040 --> 00:19:21.800
It's where the metaphor comes in so handy,

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:26.750
if your the mayor of a village,
everyone knows the village has to

00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:31.100
have a healthy economy in order to create
jobs for people to have, everybody knows

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:35.790
you gotta pay taxes that support
the school system and the police so.

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:39.350
But there is serious issues
of who to pay how much tax.

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:42.980
How transparent is the deployment
of the tax funds that are raised?

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:46.440
How competent are the organizations
that spend the tax money?

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:49.320
So we think of profits to
the share holder like that.

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:53.633
So we are very open about it, that we
don't get to create the DaVita University

00:19:53.633 --> 00:19:57.610
that our people love, with a tremendous
emphasis on personal reflection and

00:19:57.610 --> 00:20:00.150
introspection, not just applied stuff.

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:02.480
We don't get to grow and
create career opportunities.

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:06.770
We don't get to grow and create profit
sharing awards unless we are allocated

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:10.580
capital, which is just like a mayor's
gotta collect tax money in order to all

00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:14.159
the things the citizens want to exist,
the parks and the summer programs.

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:18.780
And we find that it doesn't matter, we

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:22.540
have 18,000 folks out of our 65,000 that
have not been to college and never will.

00:20:22.540 --> 00:20:26.590
They understand the concepts totally
about what real life is like,

00:20:26.590 --> 00:20:28.590
if they feel you're honest and

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:32.500
transparent about what reality is,
and are willing to have real debates.

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:35.510
So I've gotten up in front of thousands
of our teammates, and talked about my

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:41.420
compensation, an executive compensation,
with people making $17 dollars an hour.

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:45.280
And they get the right to ask questions,
or send messages.

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:50.690
So first, there's an issue of just
basic straight forward honesty,

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:53.280
and transparency, and explanation

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:57.540
to kind of loop things together in
a way that otherwise doesn't work.

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:00.590
And then second,
when we talk about tough decisions,

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:04.160
we will discuss them in
the context of a metaphor.

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:08.970
Give one example,
we made that big acquisition referred

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:13.550
to while the government was appropriating
any requires to do some divestitures.

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:17.750
And when you do the divestitures,
what the FTC requires, is that

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:22.450
you either divest all of the legacy Davita
centers or all of the new companies'.

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:25.620
You can't pick and choose because
you would cherry pick all the best

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:29.910
market positions and he can't do that,
it's a reasonable federal policy.

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:34.830
Everybody assumed,
I mean everybody assumed,

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:40.610
that I would decide to divest all
centers from the company we were buying.

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:47.770
Some of these folks would've been
in part of this, and I said no.

00:21:47.770 --> 00:21:52.530
If the other company that we're buying
has got a group that's on the ascendancy,

00:21:52.530 --> 00:21:58.090
that is a clear long-term winner, then we
will divest the legacy DaVita centers.

00:21:58.090 --> 00:22:00.370
People were appalled.

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:01.730
You know, this is a violation.

00:22:01.730 --> 00:22:04.010
We love the village,
we've been good citizens of the village.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:05.500
We have excellent clinical outcomes.

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:07.410
And you're going to sell us?

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:10.570
You don't even know who you're going to
sell it to, and we can't even switch and

00:22:10.570 --> 00:22:14.240
work because the way the FTC rules work,
you can't take all the employees.

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:16.270
You gotta,
there's a certain hands off period.

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:19.180
In our case, teammates are employees,
they call them employees.

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:20.910
And so, people are livid.

00:22:20.910 --> 00:22:25.460
And I got up in a room, a much bigger
room, with 3,000 of our leaders and

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:31.010
explained that job one of a mayor of
a village is to make sure the village is

00:22:31.010 --> 00:22:35.250
sustainable for the long term, because
every single one of us talking to them

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:36.560
is going to leave
the village at some time.

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:37.870
Because we get a great job offer,

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:40.820
because we retire, because their
spouse gets transferred, and so

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:45.580
we are all stewards for
the next set of people in these chairs.

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:48.310
And for maintaining an environment
where we can provide superb care for

00:22:48.310 --> 00:22:49.010
our patients.

00:22:49.010 --> 00:22:52.100
That's job one, and
just like if you're mayor of a village and

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:55.340
you know you've got to put a highway in or
your downtown's going to deteriorate and

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:57.300
you're going to lose
to some other village.

00:22:57.300 --> 00:23:01.100
You know you gotta rip down houses to put
in that highway, and some of those people

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:04.570
will have lived in those houses for
25 years, love their neighborhood,

00:23:04.570 --> 00:23:08.090
and you're going to rip down their
house to put in a freaking highway.

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:09.300
But that's the right thing.

00:23:09.300 --> 00:23:12.120
Either that or
the place goes dead in 30 years.

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:16.540
And got up and explained why I
had decided what I decided, and

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:21.060
about 70% of the people being divested
came up after, most of them crying,

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:26.540
saying that they accepted the logic that
the decision was best for the village, and

00:23:26.540 --> 00:23:33.110
I was consistent with my responsibility,
and they were very sad but understood.

00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:39.232
So we put it together because life.

00:23:39.232 --> 00:23:41.820
&gt;&gt; Yeah
&gt;&gt; Is hard sometimes.

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:45.670
&gt;&gt; And how much of this translates to
decision making with your core team?

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:48.770
You were talking about
the village at large.

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:51.189
But when you bring this back
to core management team,

00:23:51.189 --> 00:23:53.516
what are some of the tough
decisions you've made?

00:23:53.516 --> 00:23:59.638
Are there times where you've had to go
against the wisdom of the core team and

00:23:59.638 --> 00:24:04.724
say You know I am the mayor,
I think this is the right decision.

00:24:04.724 --> 00:24:06.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah they've been.

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:09.070
We do a lot of votes compared
to most organizations.

00:24:09.070 --> 00:24:13.040
Votes with thousands of people involved,
sometimes hundreds of people involved.

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:18.000
But I always retain the right
to overrule the vote.

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:20.170
I mean it's the discussion that matters.

00:24:20.170 --> 00:24:23.090
And the data is very useful for
me and for others but

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:28.190
in the end I have the job I have that
should come with differential insight and

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:30.700
should carry with it
a differentiated experience.

00:24:30.700 --> 00:24:35.210
Some people would question that and so

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:44.210
we had one business that I wanted to buy
and the entire team wrote against it.

00:24:44.210 --> 00:24:48.550
So I hired Baine and then they came
out and recommended against it.

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:51.310
So, we stopped that project and

00:24:52.550 --> 00:24:57.300
I pulled out of the process and
then I went home that weekend.

00:24:57.300 --> 00:25:00.865
And I just said,
our alleged vision at that point,

00:25:00.865 --> 00:25:02.230
because we were just
Kidney Care at that point,

00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:06.900
was to transform Kidney Care in America,
which we're halfway there.

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:09.960
And this was 13 years ago.

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:14.590
And we cannot seriously try to do
that unless we buy this thing,

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:16.140
even though right now it's little and

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:19.245
broken and there's no pro forma
you can do that makes sense.

00:25:19.245 --> 00:25:21.903
That we have to change a bunch
of laws to do a lot of things.

00:25:21.903 --> 00:25:27.316
And I went back on Monday and
told the team we're going to buy it and

00:25:27.316 --> 00:25:34.161
I respect all of the reasons, but it's
not inconsistent with our construct and

00:25:34.161 --> 00:25:40.802
then I guess the other part of it was
implicit in the question about core team,

00:25:40.802 --> 00:25:45.705
in the I am sensitive to the fact
that there are times that I

00:25:45.705 --> 00:25:51.830
behave in ways that are inconsistent
with our mission values.

00:25:51.830 --> 00:25:56.830
In particular with my core team,
let me say, bring it back to that.

00:25:56.830 --> 00:26:00.960
And that is where one hopes
that you scored enough

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:05.410
points in terms of intentionality,
that people forgive you for

00:26:05.410 --> 00:26:09.900
your behavioral
transactional shortcomings.

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:10.760
&gt;&gt; Do they call you on it?

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:14.550
Do they feel comfortable
enough to call you on it?

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:16.310
Do you apologize for it?

00:26:16.310 --> 00:26:17.880
How does that go?

00:26:17.880 --> 00:26:21.200
&gt;&gt; I'd say that's, I'm a bit curious
what some of the teammates say.

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:26.770
I think we're seriously above average, and

00:26:26.770 --> 00:26:29.050
people are going to be
willing to call us on it.

00:26:29.050 --> 00:26:30.860
I get scored on the core
values every year,

00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:34.630
an independent consultant goes out and
talks to a lot of folks.

00:26:34.630 --> 00:26:36.440
My scores are made public to the board and

00:26:36.440 --> 00:26:39.770
to the teammates as well
as a bunch of comments.

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:40.980
I don't get to change them.

00:26:42.030 --> 00:26:46.530
And then we'll talk about high and
low scores.

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:53.300
We take, as it's sort of self-evident,
that we all have bad stretches.

00:26:53.300 --> 00:26:55.120
That we all have faults.

00:26:55.120 --> 00:26:58.490
And that, if you can't create
an environment where you talk about them,

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:03.620
each of our personal growth
journeys are going to be stunted.

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:08.790
And so we do a lot of introspection,
a lot of reflection, a lot of discussion.

00:27:08.790 --> 00:27:12.120
And in the second part of every
executive's review is, for

00:27:12.120 --> 00:27:15.470
example when I'm reviewing others, the
first part is the most important message,

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:17.770
the second part is to go
through each core value and

00:27:17.770 --> 00:27:21.600
talk about how other people's experience,
you with respect to integrity.

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:23.870
How do they experience
you with respect to fun?

00:27:23.870 --> 00:27:26.360
How do they experience you with
respect to service excellence?

00:27:26.360 --> 00:27:30.600
Real words, and so, and
that I get the same back.

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:38.400
So I think we help me and
we help each other grow as human beings,

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:43.330
and so I think we're reasonable on that.

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:48.500
&gt;&gt; So where most of the first years been
taking your course here which we call

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:49.520
Leadership Labs.

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:54.960
One of the things we talk to them about
in the labs is being active learners and

00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:58.850
being active learners their whole lives,
their whole careers.

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:02.140
They constantly learn from what went well,
what didn't go well.

00:28:02.140 --> 00:28:03.702
Become a better manager, leader.

00:28:03.702 --> 00:28:06.940
You've spoken about some of
the ways you're getting feedback.

00:28:06.940 --> 00:28:09.760
But what are some of the other
ways that you personally

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:13.160
have worked on your own leadership and
management skill over time?

00:28:15.580 --> 00:28:22.540
&gt;&gt; Well, so, one of our pitter patters
is management is a business skill.

00:28:22.540 --> 00:28:24.440
It's really important.

00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:27.510
So you should study it and
think rigorously about it.

00:28:27.510 --> 00:28:30.910
Leadership is a human thing,
not a business thing.

00:28:30.910 --> 00:28:32.500
And if you want to get
better at leadership,

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:35.180
learn more about humans
starting with yourself.

00:28:35.180 --> 00:28:38.660
And so virtually every University class
has elements of introspection and

00:28:38.660 --> 00:28:40.280
self reflection.

00:28:40.280 --> 00:28:42.260
And in particular,

00:28:42.260 --> 00:28:47.640
if you have as many behavioral
deficiencies as I have you would

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:52.630
try to make sure not just through the 360
process but through using other tools and

00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:57.620
outside coaches that you're continuously
forced to reflect on your self.

00:28:58.940 --> 00:29:06.920
And that combined with having to discuss
yourself publicly is a tremendous spurt,

00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:13.560
thing that can spur your growth, for
me to work on some of my behavioral

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:17.908
things, I have done things like
give myself a score everyday.

00:29:17.908 --> 00:29:23.779
So when I took this job 16 years ago I was
43, when you still have serious behavioral

00:29:23.779 --> 00:29:27.271
issues, which many of you'll have, you
know, doesn't mean you're a bad person.

00:29:27.271 --> 00:29:29.082
And overall, I've got some good scores.

00:29:29.082 --> 00:29:29.870
But I had things I had to work on.

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:34.610
When you're 43 and
you've been a CEO already for eight years.

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:38.280
That means there is some stuff going on,
I mean you're not just going to get their

00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:41.790
feedback and then suddenly the next day,
stop entirely.

00:29:41.790 --> 00:29:47.530
And so for some of those things, I use the
metaphor that my brother is an alcoholic.

00:29:47.530 --> 00:29:49.460
He is now 11 years sober.

00:29:49.460 --> 00:29:51.610
And he talks about the early years,

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:55.910
he said I had to win that battle
against alcoholism every day.

00:29:55.910 --> 00:29:59.153
And so for
some of my behavioral deficiencies,

00:29:59.153 --> 00:30:01.430
like getting angry with people.

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:04.619
I started giving myself a daily score and
tracked it on a spreadsheet.

00:30:04.619 --> 00:30:09.978
Because I had to build the muscle memory
to catch myself first after I did it,

00:30:09.978 --> 00:30:13.494
and then I could go apologize
if I had the courage,

00:30:13.494 --> 00:30:16.538
and then catch myself
while I was doing it.

00:30:16.538 --> 00:30:20.200
And then catch myself right
before I was going to do it.

00:30:20.200 --> 00:30:24.038
And then after about two years
I was doing it a lot less.

00:30:24.038 --> 00:30:28.240
But if I had not kept a daily score to
confront my reality while it was still

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:33.220
fresh, then I couldn't then just sort of
consistently dismiss it as a bad day.

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:35.970
Or the person has been bugging me for
a while, and

00:30:35.970 --> 00:30:39.630
all the rationalizations get stripped
away when you score yourself everyday.

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:41.420
Did you have a drink or not?

00:30:41.420 --> 00:30:42.810
Did you have too many drinks or not?

00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:44.030
It just is the reality.

00:30:44.030 --> 00:30:45.120
It is you.

00:30:45.120 --> 00:30:46.510
It's not the external world.

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:48.870
&gt;&gt; Fascinating.

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:53.470
I want to go back a little
bit in your career.

00:30:53.470 --> 00:30:55.950
Because I think it's of
interest to the student bodies.

00:30:55.950 --> 00:31:01.020
They're thinking about where to go,
where to go next from the GSB.

00:31:01.020 --> 00:31:05.199
You did spend a good spell at Baine.

00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:09.990
There's a lot you learn
in a gig like that.

00:31:09.990 --> 00:31:13.010
And I'm wondering about the transition

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:16.480
from that to being CEO
of an operating company.

00:31:16.480 --> 00:31:19.350
What did you learn at
Baine that was helpful?

00:31:19.350 --> 00:31:22.740
Are there some things you learned
at Baine that you had to unlearn?

00:31:22.740 --> 00:31:26.490
You can talk a little bit
about the transition?

00:31:26.490 --> 00:31:31.040
&gt;&gt; First of all I have two idea about
where you should go after business school.

00:31:31.040 --> 00:31:35.660
I have one, but we'll come back to that.

00:31:35.660 --> 00:31:41.269
On the Baine versus operating company
thing, back when I graduated,

00:31:41.269 --> 00:31:45.355
which was a long time ago,
there, in that decade,

00:31:45.355 --> 00:31:50.584
in my mind there weren't really
a lot of operating companies that

00:31:50.584 --> 00:31:56.223
have thoughtful programs for
growing general managers and leaders.

00:31:56.223 --> 00:31:58.660
And that's what we're about.

00:31:58.660 --> 00:31:59.729
And that's what I wanted to become.

00:31:59.729 --> 00:32:04.970
I knew that my dream was to become
a competent general manager and

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:06.960
leader of people.

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:09.708
I knew that's what I wanted
to become proficient at.

00:32:09.708 --> 00:32:16.690
And I did not see stuff, there were jobs
out there but they weren't about that.

00:32:16.690 --> 00:32:21.330
They were just about
learning the business and

00:32:21.330 --> 00:32:25.850
then in consulting more generally,
in my, the way I would

00:32:25.850 --> 00:32:30.520
advise young people is if you want to be a
general manager, leader in the long term,

00:32:31.740 --> 00:32:36.390
that, and you find you're the sort
of person who's super bright.

00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:38.306
You figure out the case
faster than other people.

00:32:38.306 --> 00:32:44.160
You can be right verbally adroit
connected to your brain but

00:32:44.160 --> 00:32:46.130
you're not a natural team leader.

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:49.740
You're not a natural team builder.

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:52.470
You're not natural in establishing trust.

00:32:52.470 --> 00:32:55.230
Well, you got to stare at that very
realistically because if you want to be

00:32:55.230 --> 00:32:59.630
general management leader and
you have that mix relative to the world,

00:32:59.630 --> 00:33:02.840
there's no good or bad in this,
and you go to consulting or

00:33:02.840 --> 00:33:05.970
investment banking,
you're existing muscles get way,

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:10.790
way stronger and your pay goes up,
and these other muscles stay weak.

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:13.270
And then you want to make a shift and
it's a nightmare.

00:33:13.270 --> 00:33:17.800
Because you got to take a huge cut in pay
and work on your remedial capabilities.

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:22.040
So, you don't do it, and I got a lot
of friends who are 59, and wealthy and

00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:27.480
not happy, because they went too much,
they didn't have a good strategy.

00:33:27.480 --> 00:33:33.060
Similarly, if you're great at project
management and team leadership and

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:38.130
the rest, which I felt for me,
that was some of my natural sport for

00:33:38.130 --> 00:33:43.070
me, a lot to learn but
a natural sport but you need help and

00:33:43.070 --> 00:33:46.380
more strategic discipline,
more analytical discipline.

00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:49.290
You need to just be exposed to a lot more.

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:52.460
Then it goes the other way,
that going there and

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:56.020
getting that incredible exposure and
learning those disciplines.

00:33:56.020 --> 00:33:58.800
And then, you transfer over to a sport
you were, sort of, born to play.

00:34:00.830 --> 00:34:03.780
And, so I think it's very, there,
there's no generic answer.

00:34:03.780 --> 00:34:08.340
It's very person specific and it
requires incredible intellectual honesty

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:10.530
about your strengths and weaknesses.

00:34:10.530 --> 00:34:12.640
And where you want to end up.

00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:16.920
And so, for me I needed,
I needed exposure.

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:21.290
I needed to run with that sort
of intellect with people for

00:34:21.290 --> 00:34:25.300
a while to make me sharper, help me grow.

00:34:25.300 --> 00:34:27.810
I needed to become more disciplined,
analytically.

00:34:27.810 --> 00:34:31.145
I needed all those things,
and I got them, big time.

00:34:31.145 --> 00:34:32.070
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:35.929
&gt;&gt; And then went to play where I always
knew, I mean, the sport I wanted to play.

00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:38.370
Thank you.

00:34:38.370 --> 00:34:41.110
I don't want to,
I don't want to hog all your time.

00:34:41.110 --> 00:34:47.040
Let's open it up for, for
questions from the audience.

00:34:48.560 --> 00:34:51.240
&gt;&gt; I have the first question from twitter.

00:34:51.240 --> 00:34:55.580
How do you distinguish company as
community from company as a family.

00:34:55.580 --> 00:34:57.990
How does one, for instance,
fire a community member?

00:34:59.120 --> 00:34:59.980
Yeah.

00:34:59.980 --> 00:35:05.730
Actually, when you asked about
tough moments, firing a friend.

00:35:05.730 --> 00:35:11.780
And in some cases, someone you literally
love is a brutal thing to have to do.

00:35:12.830 --> 00:35:18.270
And particularly if they love the village,
and you have that personal relationship.

00:35:18.270 --> 00:35:19.640
And then you tell them they have to go.

00:35:23.900 --> 00:35:25.400
And we used community,

00:35:25.400 --> 00:35:30.740
which if course is made up of families,
very explicitly because the family thing,

00:35:30.740 --> 00:35:33.940
while that level of intimacy exists
in some of neighborhoods and

00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:38.190
some of our smaller teams, for us,
some of my senior executive team,

00:35:38.190 --> 00:35:43.580
we love each other as we love our oldest,
dearest friends.

00:35:43.580 --> 00:35:44.920
And we talk about it.

00:35:44.920 --> 00:35:49.920
And so we are almost like families.

00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:52.470
But for the bigger community,
that would be an overly aggressive word.

00:35:52.470 --> 00:35:56.250
It would just be inaccurate to
say the entire 65,000 people

00:35:56.250 --> 00:35:58.290
across 12 countries is a family.

00:35:58.290 --> 00:36:00.500
It's just this,
it would be an inappropriate metaphor.

00:36:01.740 --> 00:36:04.350
The, just like a village has
families within it, that's,

00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:05.560
that's just more accurate.

00:36:05.560 --> 00:36:07.970
We're serious about the metaphor,
if you're going to, it's going to,

00:36:07.970 --> 00:36:11.520
if it's going to help you make decisions
and help you talk about decisions, and

00:36:11.520 --> 00:36:14.120
help people understand how you want
to behave, It's gotta be real,

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:18.240
it's gotta make sense, it's gotta be
internally holistically consistent.

00:36:18.240 --> 00:36:21.090
And so, the one's there, we,
I'm much better at this,

00:36:21.090 --> 00:36:24.120
I used to be bad at giving
people bad reviews and

00:36:24.120 --> 00:36:28.180
bad at terminating because I was
too intent on proving I was right.

00:36:28.180 --> 00:36:30.200
I'm right about the ways you underperform.

00:36:30.200 --> 00:36:32.810
I'm right about it's the right
decision to terminate you.

00:36:32.810 --> 00:36:36.000
And then one of my executives who work for
me,

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:40.910
a guy named Tom Musselton, taught me
what I thought about conceptually but

00:36:40.910 --> 00:36:44.160
have never applied to that area which
is the holy script of intentionality.

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:49.420
Until I got to the point where I
intended to be helpful to that person in

00:36:49.420 --> 00:36:55.310
the review or in the decision, helpful, I
could never become very good at doing it.

00:36:55.310 --> 00:36:59.090
And as I went through my year or
two of learning that from this gentleman,

00:36:59.090 --> 00:37:02.500
there were times I cancelled the meeting
because I knew I was still going in

00:37:02.500 --> 00:37:05.860
with my old tendency wanting
to prove I was right.

00:37:05.860 --> 00:37:09.680
And take care and respond to any rebuttal.

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:15.050
And so if you go into it with the right
intention in your heart, your head will do

00:37:15.050 --> 00:37:21.390
just fine by itself and in a village you
gotta maintain the health of the village.

00:37:21.390 --> 00:37:23.920
Doesn't meant they're
going to be destroyed.

00:37:23.920 --> 00:37:26.070
There are other jobs,
there are other villages.

00:37:26.070 --> 00:37:30.640
In many cases, there are people who won't
take a demotion, and so in that case, it

00:37:30.640 --> 00:37:35.730
becomes their own decision to go, in terms
of a majority of successful executives

00:37:35.730 --> 00:37:40.790
have a really hard time with a demotion,
I think that's totally philosophically

00:37:40.790 --> 00:37:46.870
inappropriate and shallow, but
it's where most human beings come from.

00:37:46.870 --> 00:37:49.050
Shallow's not the right word, but

00:37:49.050 --> 00:37:52.600
I think in many cases they
would have been happier.

00:37:52.600 --> 00:37:54.760
But I think putting it
in the right context,

00:37:54.760 --> 00:37:59.138
having the right intentionality, and
then being respectful and humane.

00:37:59.138 --> 00:38:04.970
&gt;&gt; My name's Fernanda.

00:38:04.970 --> 00:38:11.550
I would like to hear a little more about
the intentional parts of a culture so

00:38:11.550 --> 00:38:15.780
in the beginning here you were saying
oh what we just did that was not my

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:20.290
intent when they started off and I'm sure
a lot of things evolved through the years.

00:38:20.290 --> 00:38:24.540
But I would like to hear about your
vision in the very beginning and

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:27.770
your role as a leader in
making that vision come true.

00:38:29.400 --> 00:38:33.980
Let me take a stab at it and then you
come back if I don't hit it right.

00:38:33.980 --> 00:38:39.140
Intentionality is fundamental and
we just had 10 people

00:38:39.140 --> 00:38:44.070
last night in Denver who just
went through something that I'll

00:38:44.070 --> 00:38:48.570
describe as part of our intentionality and
that is the first word they used.

00:38:48.570 --> 00:38:51.660
A couple of big groups that
are thinking of joining us.

00:38:51.660 --> 00:38:55.510
And when that happens, we just invite
them to come to our big meetings.

00:38:55.510 --> 00:38:58.270
So, a part A and a part B.

00:38:58.270 --> 00:39:01.230
First, when I showed up, and

00:39:01.230 --> 00:39:06.050
for reasons we can go into later perhaps,
there was a big family decision,

00:39:07.310 --> 00:39:12.750
I did announce to the groups
like this that That number one,

00:39:13.970 --> 00:39:19.130
I wanted to create a deferentially
healthy place for people to work and have

00:39:19.130 --> 00:39:22.000
an unusual percentage of people say that
it was the best place they ever worked.

00:39:22.000 --> 00:39:26.420
Not perfect, not easy, but
the best place they ever worked.

00:39:26.420 --> 00:39:29.190
And number two is we wanted to
build the greatest dialysis company

00:39:29.190 --> 00:39:30.630
the world had ever seen.

00:39:30.630 --> 00:39:32.590
Then that is what I said.

00:39:32.590 --> 00:39:35.970
And we were on the verge of bankruptcy,
we're being investigate by the SCC,

00:39:35.970 --> 00:39:37.960
we're being sued by shareholders.

00:39:37.960 --> 00:39:39.537
We had no COO, no CIO,

00:39:39.537 --> 00:39:44.434
no CFO, we were in six weeks of
missing payroll of 9,000 people.

00:39:44.434 --> 00:39:47.371
And so
when I said those words in that situation,

00:39:47.371 --> 00:39:51.753
a lot people thought it was inappropriate
emphasis given where we were.

00:39:51.753 --> 00:39:54.017
In my mind, if you care about that stuff,

00:39:54.017 --> 00:39:56.426
the time to start working
at it is right now.

00:39:56.426 --> 00:39:58.238
Either so you'd never delay.

00:39:58.238 --> 00:40:02.078
It doesn't mean you don't have to do all
the other stuff, but there's no such thing

00:40:02.078 --> 00:40:04.960
the leaders lead, they don't wait for
the right time to lead.

00:40:06.390 --> 00:40:09.090
Second when I did that,
if you use this room,

00:40:09.090 --> 00:40:12.320
a third of the people thought
it was just manipulation.

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:16.600
Superficial marketing, hoity toity MBA,

00:40:16.600 --> 00:40:19.288
wants us to stay,
looks like we're going to go bankrupt.

00:40:19.288 --> 00:40:23.200
So this is like, so if he wants us to say,
he shouldn't give us

00:40:23.200 --> 00:40:27.570
superficial football speeches like that,
he should give us retention bonuses.

00:40:27.570 --> 00:40:28.680
And then maybe a third of the group,

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:30.600
obviously you don't
know the right numbers.

00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:34.930
Were thinking well,
that's very nice, he seems sincere,

00:40:34.930 --> 00:40:36.510
but that'll last about a month.

00:40:37.580 --> 00:40:42.200
And then maybe a third of the group
said wow, I would like to work in

00:40:42.200 --> 00:40:46.530
a place that's differentially a healthy
place for human beings to work.

00:40:46.530 --> 00:40:51.170
And has a dream of actually making
a significant impact on the world.

00:40:51.170 --> 00:40:55.380
I'd like that, I'm hoping he's sincere and
he's competent.

00:40:55.380 --> 00:40:59.877
And if you've not done it yet,
given a speech in a room or

00:40:59.877 --> 00:41:05.800
two-thirds of the people,
think you're full of it, it's not fun.

00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:07.690
But it demonstrates intentionality.

00:41:07.690 --> 00:41:10.560
And then you say, and
by the way I know most the people in

00:41:10.560 --> 00:41:14.900
the room think this is not pragmatic or
not sincere or naive or whatever.

00:41:14.900 --> 00:41:19.789
And then they go okay, so
he actually knows that a bunch of us,

00:41:19.789 --> 00:41:23.570
think it's bias and he's still saying it.

00:41:23.570 --> 00:41:24.740
Now that's interesting.

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:28.770
Because at first we thought he was dumb or
naive, now we see he actually knows it and

00:41:28.770 --> 00:41:30.790
he's still saying it.

00:41:30.790 --> 00:41:34.310
And then people would of course say, how?

00:41:34.310 --> 00:41:36.710
It's okay, that's what we
have to figure out together.

00:41:36.710 --> 00:41:40.546
Here's some ideas about our
policies around the university,

00:41:40.546 --> 00:41:43.020
here's some of our policy about
helping teammates in need.

00:41:43.020 --> 00:41:46.440
Here's some of brainstorming here and
there on personal and

00:41:46.440 --> 00:41:48.860
professional development and
impact on communities.

00:41:48.860 --> 00:41:53.790
And so we started to demonstrate by
having regular meetings where the only

00:41:53.790 --> 00:41:54.950
subject was.

00:41:54.950 --> 00:41:57.519
How do we make this notion of being
a differentially healthy place to

00:41:57.519 --> 00:41:58.690
work come true.

00:41:58.690 --> 00:42:00.970
And just like if you had
a marketing problem or

00:42:00.970 --> 00:42:04.470
a manufacturing problem
it was the agenda item.

00:42:04.470 --> 00:42:07.043
You would generate ideas,
you would allocate resources,

00:42:07.043 --> 00:42:09.680
you would have programs,
you would monitor the execution.

00:42:09.680 --> 00:42:12.150
So we respected the challenge and

00:42:12.150 --> 00:42:15.380
we demonstrated that we
were damn serious about it.

00:42:15.380 --> 00:42:17.290
And we used to get on the phone,
as we still do it.

00:42:17.290 --> 00:42:19.110
Every eight weeks for
a voice of the village call,

00:42:19.110 --> 00:42:22.800
I just had one yesterday,
about 5,000 people across the world.

00:42:22.800 --> 00:42:23.770
And in those days,

00:42:23.770 --> 00:42:27.130
every call I'd say what is the incremental
evidence since the last call?

00:42:27.130 --> 00:42:31.250
To suggest we're serious about
this mission and values stuff.

00:42:31.250 --> 00:42:33.760
And so to put it way, way out there and

00:42:33.760 --> 00:42:36.920
then at some point,
you call the question and say okay.

00:42:37.960 --> 00:42:40.360
We call it crossing the bridge and
we have a bridge in every neighborhood.

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:42.490
I walk across the bridge every morning.

00:42:42.490 --> 00:42:45.170
And after you've been with us six months,

00:42:45.170 --> 00:42:49.700
we ask you to stand at that bridge and
decide, you never have to tell us.

00:42:49.700 --> 00:42:51.530
And if you decide to walk across it,

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:54.940
it means that you've declared
that you share accountability.

00:42:54.940 --> 00:42:58.030
For creating a differentially
healthy place to work,

00:42:58.030 --> 00:42:59.430
having us be a community first.

00:42:59.430 --> 00:43:03.010
And trying to have a differential
impact on health care in the world.

00:43:03.010 --> 00:43:06.580
And so it was very explicit,

00:43:06.580 --> 00:43:11.250
it was very much reinforced by actual
actions, actual intellectual investment.

00:43:11.250 --> 00:43:14.640
Actual project management,
operating follow through.

00:43:14.640 --> 00:43:17.790
Actual honest discussions
about how we were doing.

00:43:17.790 --> 00:43:21.830
Because back then we had 9,000
people across 450 locations.

00:43:21.830 --> 00:43:24.010
If you didn't get way intense,

00:43:24.010 --> 00:43:26.640
it's not like being able to
call everybody in a room.

00:43:26.640 --> 00:43:27.940
It had to be much more serious.

00:43:27.940 --> 00:43:30.280
So I think those are some of the things

00:43:30.280 --> 00:43:32.760
that really hammered
home the intentionality.

00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:35.183
And got a lot people
thinking about whether or

00:43:35.183 --> 00:43:37.172
not they wanted to cross the bridge.

00:43:37.172 --> 00:43:41.660
And then I'll throw out one other
thing that was powerful part of it.

00:43:41.660 --> 00:43:44.760
A bunch of managers in the first
year crossed the bridge.

00:43:44.760 --> 00:43:49.050
They said whoa,
we believe this and we're psyched.

00:43:49.050 --> 00:43:51.590
It would be great to retire and
say I tried to build

00:43:51.590 --> 00:43:55.420
the greatest douse this company will
ever seen and have a differentially.

00:43:55.420 --> 00:43:59.260
I'm on board, but when I go back to
Tulsa or New york or whatever and

00:43:59.260 --> 00:44:02.620
talk to the 25 people in my little center.

00:44:02.620 --> 00:44:04.030
I can't bring that alive and

00:44:04.030 --> 00:44:08.036
were open six days a week, three shifts
a day I can't even pull my team together.

00:44:08.036 --> 00:44:12.540
Much less convey it all and the videos
help but they're not enough and so

00:44:12.540 --> 00:44:16.530
the village concept is
stopping at the manager level.

00:44:16.530 --> 00:44:20.060
And so we had town halls and
their managers saying this to me and

00:44:20.060 --> 00:44:22.320
the other senior leaders.

00:44:22.320 --> 00:44:24.800
And we said okay,
how can we break through that?

00:44:24.800 --> 00:44:28.430
And then somebody had
the temerity to say well,

00:44:28.430 --> 00:44:33.260
we couldn't actually fly
all our people somewhere.

00:44:33.260 --> 00:44:36.450
To go to meetings like we do and
discuss this stuff.

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:39.430
And of course,
no you can't because it's expensive and

00:44:39.430 --> 00:44:43.380
because it's really hard to get substitute
staffing in all the local centers.

00:44:43.380 --> 00:44:48.450
And then we said okay, but it appears
we can't do what we say we want to do.

00:44:48.450 --> 00:44:52.080
The dream is not feasible unless we're
willing to do something that we don't have

00:44:52.080 --> 00:44:53.270
anyone else that does.

00:44:53.270 --> 00:44:56.390
And so ever since that time,
we have academies that are two days long.

00:44:56.390 --> 00:44:57.820
Every teammate in America and

00:44:57.820 --> 00:45:00.690
now or pretty soon the world is
invited to come for two days.

00:45:00.690 --> 00:45:03.260
We have people who've never been
on a business trip, lots of them.

00:45:03.260 --> 00:45:05.650
Some have never been on a plane,
don't have credit cards,

00:45:05.650 --> 00:45:07.440
never checked into a hotel.

00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:10.540
And then the vice president's right
next to the doctor who's right next to

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:14.800
the tech who's right
next to the secretary.

00:45:14.800 --> 00:45:17.330
And it's two days with
no applied training.

00:45:17.330 --> 00:45:19.190
It's all about our mission, our vision,

00:45:19.190 --> 00:45:23.990
our language, our rituals, people go
through things like Myers-Briggs and

00:45:23.990 --> 00:45:26.060
we have thousands of people
who've never done that.

00:45:26.060 --> 00:45:28.840
And the second day we ask every
one to write their personal credo,

00:45:28.840 --> 00:45:31.550
which is essentially
a statement of life purpose.

00:45:31.550 --> 00:45:35.190
And then think about,
are they living a life consistent

00:45:35.190 --> 00:45:38.670
with what they would say their values and
life mission is?

00:45:38.670 --> 00:45:40.650
And then do we fit in it?

00:45:40.650 --> 00:45:44.800
And so when we started doing that,
when we told people across America.

00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:46.760
We will fly you for
two days of discussion and

00:45:46.760 --> 00:45:50.660
have senior executives
there to help interact.

00:45:50.660 --> 00:45:55.470
That was a huge piece of data for
people that we were serious.

00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:57.636
And still all along the way,

00:45:57.636 --> 00:46:02.607
you had to go through waves of skepticism,
cynicism, fatigue.

00:46:02.607 --> 00:46:07.232
You set a tough budget and
does that mean it's not a village,

00:46:07.232 --> 00:46:09.880
was that reasonably responsive?

00:46:09.880 --> 00:46:13.940
&gt;&gt; I think we have time for
one more, okay?

00:46:13.940 --> 00:46:16.245
&gt;&gt; Can a non citizen call you KT?

00:46:16.245 --> 00:46:17.267
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:46:17.267 --> 00:46:18.846
&gt;&gt; It's a pleasure having you, KT.

00:46:18.846 --> 00:46:20.945
&gt;&gt; It depends what words are in front or
after it.

00:46:20.945 --> 00:46:25.720
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Can you speak a little
bit more about the disadvantages to

00:46:25.720 --> 00:46:29.530
having that strong of a culture,
specifically for your leadership role?

00:46:29.530 --> 00:46:33.320
And on a more personal note for
you and for your citizens,

00:46:33.320 --> 00:46:38.090
how does having your daytime
job being that touchy feely?

00:46:38.090 --> 00:46:42.633
How does that impact your life at home and
your relationship with your own family?

00:46:42.633 --> 00:46:43.399
&gt;&gt; Whoa.

00:46:43.399 --> 00:46:47.670
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; On the first one,

00:46:49.110 --> 00:46:52.150
which I think was how
does the strength and

00:46:52.150 --> 00:46:57.730
nature of the culture constitute or
it is a disadvantage?

00:46:58.740 --> 00:47:03.910
I've not been asked that specific

00:47:03.910 --> 00:47:08.420
question and thinking spontaneous,
I really coming up empty.

00:47:08.420 --> 00:47:14.490
I think it's always a good thing.

00:47:14.490 --> 00:47:16.650
Now some days it makes some stuff harder,

00:47:16.650 --> 00:47:21.290
because you have to explain really
delicate things that are uncomfortable.

00:47:21.290 --> 00:47:24.220
But that's sort of like the bridge
you have to pass if you want

00:47:24.220 --> 00:47:27.540
a special culture is that's inevitable.

00:47:27.540 --> 00:47:30.980
So I think it forces
uncomfortable human behavior.

00:47:30.980 --> 00:47:33.370
It forces you to do stuff and

00:47:33.370 --> 00:47:36.870
me to do stuff I might not have
the courage to do otherwise.

00:47:36.870 --> 00:47:39.992
One of the things we tell young
leaders is get out there,

00:47:39.992 --> 00:47:44.420
talk about your values out loud
to the people that work for you.

00:47:44.420 --> 00:47:47.719
And it didnt take us a bunch of them
would be looking and saying [SOUND].

00:47:47.719 --> 00:47:50.154
You think you live that value?

00:47:50.154 --> 00:47:54.018
I'm not so persuaded and
the power of speaking out loud, so

00:47:54.018 --> 00:47:58.270
I think it forces moments of
serious discomfort, but it's never

00:47:58.270 --> 00:48:03.700
a A disadvantage would be my pretty strong
reaction, although I get the question.

00:48:03.700 --> 00:48:05.810
I'm not trying to be dismissive.

00:48:05.810 --> 00:48:09.980
And then in the second one is

00:48:09.980 --> 00:48:14.450
particularly thought provoking considering
my son, my little munchkin is in the room.

00:48:17.250 --> 00:48:22.482
But I believe that the DaVita village

00:48:22.482 --> 00:48:27.236
has made me a better human being.

00:48:27.236 --> 00:48:33.040
And I think obviously my kids have

00:48:33.040 --> 00:48:37.690
limited memories, because they were ten
and eight when we started this adventure.

00:48:39.950 --> 00:48:44.590
But other people who know me
throughout would talk about how it's

00:48:45.750 --> 00:48:51.390
fostered first, encouraged,
motivated growth as a human being,

00:48:51.390 --> 00:48:56.120
and I love most the letters we
get from people that have left.

00:48:56.120 --> 00:48:59.100
Or when we give core value awards,
which we take very very seriously,

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:00.520
one of the rituals of our village.

00:49:00.520 --> 00:49:01.910
The annual core value words.

00:49:01.910 --> 00:49:05.520
And then they happen throughout most of
the year are very, very serious events.

00:49:05.520 --> 00:49:08.370
There's no recognition on our night of
honor for business accomplishments.

00:49:08.370 --> 00:49:10.490
It's all human behavior.

00:49:11.780 --> 00:49:16.260
That often, for me the most special ones,
and often the remarks of the people who

00:49:16.260 --> 00:49:21.040
get it will say that I've been at DaVita
six years and I'm a better daddy.

00:49:21.040 --> 00:49:23.580
I'm a better mommy, I'm a better daughter.

00:49:24.600 --> 00:49:26.020
That's what we're about.

00:49:26.020 --> 00:49:29.320
For us, our culture's not a part
of our business strategy.

00:49:29.320 --> 00:49:33.980
Our culture is about our dreams
of what life should be like.

00:49:33.980 --> 00:49:37.999
And it just happens to be inserted
into a capitalistic enterprise.

00:49:39.980 --> 00:49:45.914
So I feel it's, it's why I'm still,
as my son will tell you, highly imperfect.

00:49:45.914 --> 00:49:51.246
I think it's made me way
more mindful at home.

00:49:51.246 --> 00:49:56.196
And it's been demanding
as some of you know

00:49:56.196 --> 00:50:00.732
back 16 years ago when I took the job,

00:50:00.732 --> 00:50:06.890
I had just come off a private
equity backed thing.

00:50:06.890 --> 00:50:11.190
I ran a company, it got taken over,
it was very in need financially and

00:50:11.190 --> 00:50:14.270
really sad because we thought we
were building something special.

00:50:14.270 --> 00:50:18.850
Then I did a private equity backed thing
with TPG and Bain Capital and it failed.

00:50:18.850 --> 00:50:22.180
And it failed largely because of mistakes
I made, strategically and otherwise.

00:50:22.180 --> 00:50:25.730
And I had friends who moved their
families across the country

00:50:25.730 --> 00:50:27.590
to join this company with me.

00:50:27.590 --> 00:50:30.460
And then a year and a half later, I had to
tell them their job was being eliminated.

00:50:30.460 --> 00:50:35.620
And we ended up selling the piece of
the company, losing half the money, and

00:50:35.620 --> 00:50:41.470
in the face of that, so I was working
very hard to try to save it and didn't.

00:50:41.470 --> 00:50:47.290
And so when that was over, I told my
wife and kids that I was going to

00:50:47.290 --> 00:50:53.940
take six months off and spend time with
them and get healthier and the rest.

00:50:53.940 --> 00:50:58.330
And when search firms would call,
I would say, call me in six months,

00:50:58.330 --> 00:50:59.670
I'm going to take some downtime.

00:50:59.670 --> 00:51:04.650
I was within eight weeks of being done and
then they called for this company.

00:51:04.650 --> 00:51:08.440
And since I had run one of their
caregiving company before that was

00:51:08.440 --> 00:51:13.260
the most professionally fulfilling
time in my life, and so

00:51:13.260 --> 00:51:18.820
I went through a hellatious month with my
wife, Denise, who was also an HBS grad,

00:51:18.820 --> 00:51:22.010
was one of America's first
female venture capital partners,

00:51:22.010 --> 00:51:25.840
about how much of my
taking this job was ego.

00:51:25.840 --> 00:51:28.090
want to make some more money,
want to prove I can turn a company,

00:51:28.090 --> 00:51:29.340
want to prove I can run a bigger company.

00:51:29.340 --> 00:51:32.290
Even though it was in another city,
it was a total turn around.

00:51:32.290 --> 00:51:34.600
My family wasn't going to move.

00:51:34.600 --> 00:51:36.860
Versus how much of it was virtuous and

00:51:36.860 --> 00:51:39.260
had to do with having
a positive effect on the world.

00:51:39.260 --> 00:51:41.440
And I wasn't sure about the answer.

00:51:41.440 --> 00:51:46.530
I knew it was a bunch of both and
clearly we decided what we did and

00:51:46.530 --> 00:51:52.020
it was sort of pivotal in the end,
was saying, the fact that

00:51:52.020 --> 00:51:56.580
it was going to be difficult was highly
relevant, but it's one thing to be home

00:51:56.580 --> 00:52:01.110
five nights a week with your kids and talk
to them about leading a purposeful life,

00:52:01.110 --> 00:52:04.690
and a whole another thing to be
there fewer nights, but lead one.

00:52:04.690 --> 00:52:10.990
And that's the gift that The Village gave
me and so, while it's pulled me away,

00:52:10.990 --> 00:52:15.630
and certainly there's times I've been
distracted, that I think my kids know that

00:52:15.630 --> 00:52:20.710
I'm doing my best to lead a purposeful
life and become a mindful person.

00:52:20.710 --> 00:52:23.465
And I think that counts for
something with them, I don't know.

00:52:23.465 --> 00:52:25.922
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We'll check that out with

00:52:25.922 --> 00:52:26.815
a munchkin later.

00:52:26.815 --> 00:52:31.097
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; All of the students who are here in

00:52:31.097 --> 00:52:36.923
order to get into the GSB had to answer
a question which has become customary for

00:52:36.923 --> 00:52:41.490
us to ask I guess,
which is what matters most to you and why?

00:52:41.490 --> 00:52:46.442
&gt;&gt; I hate questions like that.

00:52:48.540 --> 00:52:53.590
At DaVita people some times say, well what
matters more keeping the profits up and

00:52:53.590 --> 00:52:57.190
the stock price up or the village?

00:52:57.190 --> 00:52:58.900
I say it's a mirror
it's a false dichotomy,

00:52:58.900 --> 00:53:01.670
it's like what's more
important to you air or water?

00:53:01.670 --> 00:53:02.690
You have to have both.

00:53:02.690 --> 00:53:05.910
To say one's more important
than the other is,

00:53:05.910 --> 00:53:09.460
I think, a paradigm that defies reality.

00:53:09.460 --> 00:53:12.360
And so I would say three.

00:53:12.360 --> 00:53:19.270
One is there is nothing more special
than the people you love and love you.

00:53:19.270 --> 00:53:22.460
And the older you get,
I'm a couple months from 60,

00:53:22.460 --> 00:53:28.100
the more significant that he is and
what you shall sow, you shall reap.

00:53:28.100 --> 00:53:33.190
By the time you get to my age,
a lot of stuff is locked and loaded

00:53:33.190 --> 00:53:36.940
in terms of the depth and the breadth of
your friendships, and the texture of those

00:53:36.940 --> 00:53:42.159
friendships and relationships with your
family, and you kids, your spouse.

00:53:43.410 --> 00:53:47.220
Second is to try to become

00:53:48.310 --> 00:53:53.890
more mindful each day, to tread gently
on the planet and the people in it.

00:53:53.890 --> 00:53:56.020
This is a particularly difficult one for
me.

00:53:58.390 --> 00:54:01.707
But there's Buddhist saying
that's strictly speaking,

00:54:01.707 --> 00:54:05.630
there are no spiritual people,
there's only spiritual practices.

00:54:05.630 --> 00:54:10.189
And people who act in a humane,
mindful, gentle way every day,

00:54:10.189 --> 00:54:15.266
all day, send amazing human ripples
through the course of their life.

00:54:15.266 --> 00:54:17.680
And so for
me that's a particular challenge and

00:54:17.680 --> 00:54:21.730
I'm proud of the progress I've made,
because it's so important.

00:54:21.730 --> 00:54:25.910
And then third is the more macro,
what impact do you have on the world?

00:54:25.910 --> 00:54:30.468
And I'll tell you for people in this
room you have spectacular capabilities.

00:54:30.468 --> 00:54:35.380
Spectacular experiences and
spectacular luck in getting to come

00:54:35.380 --> 00:54:38.510
to a place like this with people
like you're sitting with.

00:54:38.510 --> 00:54:44.430
And so I sincerely hope that many of you
take on the incremental responsibility

00:54:44.430 --> 00:54:49.410
on top of the first two of trying to
have a significant impact on the world.

00:54:50.620 --> 00:54:52.740
&gt;&gt; Great answer on all three points.

00:54:52.740 --> 00:54:56.310
Do you have any closing thoughts
you'd like to share with this crowd?

00:54:57.990 --> 00:55:00.390
&gt;&gt; Well, for me, honored to be here.

00:55:00.390 --> 00:55:04.172
When I was in those seats from 1981 to 83,

00:55:04.172 --> 00:55:08.806
it was my dream that maybe one
day I would get to come back and

00:55:08.806 --> 00:55:13.645
talk to, excuse me,
kids like you about important topics.

00:55:13.645 --> 00:55:17.965
So for me, getting to be here and be
here with Garth and Charles O'Reilly and

00:55:17.965 --> 00:55:20.137
Joel Peterson, this is like a dream.

00:55:20.137 --> 00:55:24.058
So I really encourage young people,
think about your dreams and

00:55:24.058 --> 00:55:27.396
don't give up on them,
because sometimes they come true,

00:55:27.396 --> 00:55:31.970
they're rarely easy, and they are rarely
linear to go after, but for me,

00:55:31.970 --> 00:55:36.719
many of the dreams I had when I was your
age have come true, and I feel so lucky.

00:55:36.719 --> 00:55:41.327
And the second thing I would say
is just with all the ambitions and

00:55:41.327 --> 00:55:46.789
the pressures of two career couples and
having babies and through all that,

00:55:46.789 --> 00:55:52.334
to really savor the adventure of life,
there's a French author, Colette,

00:55:52.334 --> 00:55:57.486
who said I had a wonderful life,
I only wished I'd realized it sooner.

00:55:57.486 --> 00:55:59.228
&gt;&gt; Please join me in thanking KT.

00:55:59.228 --> 00:56:02.196
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:56:02.196 --> 00:56:07.503
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

